  "details": "Hardware\n\nSamsung Galaxy Book (Intel Core Ultra 5, Lunar Lake)\n\nWindows 11 Home\n\nBuilt-in camera: Intel(R) LNL AV Stream Camera (Intel MIPI/IPU)\n\nIssue\n\nMy laptop camera works perfectly in every application.\n\nHowever, the moment I enable Selfie Expression (Desktop Mode → Settings → Tracking & IK), the camera feed immediately turns solid purple/magenta. My face is no longer visible, so tracking never starts.\n\nThe strange part is that the issue persists after closing VRChat. The camera remains purple in every application (Windows Camera, OBS, Discord, etc.), and the only way to restore it is to reboot the entire computer.\n\nBecause the problem affects the camera system-wide until a reboot, it seems like VRChat may be putting the Intel MIPI/IPU camera into a bad state and not resetting it properly.\n\nThings I've already tried\n\nReinstalling and rolling back the camera driver\n\nChanging Selfie Expression quality settings\n\nEnabling/disabling Auto Adjust\n\nUsing OBS Virtual Camera (it also shows the purple image because the physical camera is already broken)\n\nTesting a separate USB webcam (unfortunately that webcam itself was defective, so I couldn't compare)\n\nQuestions\n\nHas anyone else with an Intel Core Ultra (Lunar Lake) laptop and an Intel MIPI/IPU camera experienced this with Selfie Expression?\n\nIs there a known workaround, such as disabling Windows Studio Effects, Intel AI Boost/NPU features, or using a specific camera driver version?\n\nDoes this appear to be a VRChat bug, an Intel camera driver bug, or an interaction between the two?\n\nAny information or suggestions would be greatly appreciated. Thanks!",
